<div class="policy-explorer" data-policy-explorer>
  <div>
    <p class="example-label">Illustrative policy · fictional organisation</p>
    <h3>Change the request. See the decision.</h3>
    <p>
      This teaching example runs only in your browser. It neither grants permission nor submits
      a request. Your platform’s trusted policy and current identity make the real decision.
    </p>
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Example application request</legend>
      <label>
        Requester’s access
        <select name="membership">
          <option value="current">Current member of owning team</option>
          <option value="other">Different team</option>
          <option value="revoked">Revoked membership</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label>
        Hosting target
        <select name="runtime">
          <option value="workers">Ordinary Workers</option>
          <option value="kubernetes">Kubernetes candidate</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label>
        Data requirement
        <select name="location">
          <option value="standard">Approved synthetic / internal data</option>
          <option value="australia">Strict Australian-only handling</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label>
        Change
        <select name="change">
          <option value="routine">Routine supported update</option>
          <option value="exception">Exception to standing policy</option>
        </select>
      </label>
    </fieldset>
  </div>
  <div class="policy-result" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
    <p class="example-label">Example outcome</p>
    <h3 data-decision>Allowed</h3>
    <p data-reason>
      The current team member’s supported Workers update fits this example’s standing policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      {" "}<strong>Next:</strong>{" "}
      {" "}<span data-next>
        A trusted runner would recheck identity, candidate and policy before any provider
        effect, then verify the service independently.
      </span>{" "}
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Real-world authority stays with the server and selected runner. A downloaded plan, this
      example and a provider acknowledgement cannot authorise a release.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
<noscript>
  <p>
    The interactive example needs JavaScript. The rules are: other-team or revoked access is
    denied; an unqualified target or unsubstantiated Australian-only requirement is
    unsupported; an exception requires approval; a routine supported update is allowed. These
    are illustrative rules only.
  </p>
</noscript>
